WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.380
Stretched dishes show more tracked divisions.

2
00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:06.220
What else, besides stretch, changed in those dishes?

3
00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:12.260
Chapter one locked three things: the prediction, endpoint and independent unit.

4
00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.140
A control is how you find out.

5
00:00:18.567 --> 00:00:20.527
Look at what the stretched dish went through.

6
00:00:21.707 --> 00:00:25.747
Someone assembled the device, mounted it and carried it to the microscope.

7
00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:31.387
If the control dish skipped those steps, stretch now includes every handling

8
00:00:31.387 --> 00:00:37.407
difference. So unstretched devices are assembled, mounted and imaged identically.

9
00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:40.907
The medium raises the same problem.

10
00:00:42.287 --> 00:00:48.167
Wnt3A medium comes from mouse L cells engineered to make Wnt3A.

11
00:00:49.387 --> 00:00:54.047
The matched control is medium from the parental L cells, prepared the same way.

12
00:00:55.247 --> 00:00:59.207
Even that cannot promise Wnt3A is the only difference.

13
00:01:00.317 --> 00:01:02.497
So add a readout of the pathway itself.

14
00:01:04.077 --> 00:01:09.837
TOPdGFP is a short-lived green reporter driven by beta-catenin-responsive sites.

15
00:01:11.197 --> 00:01:16.797
Its signal asks whether the Wnt medium engaged beta-catenin-dependent transcription

16
00:01:16.797 --> 00:01:21.977
in these cells. If it stays flat, the medium may not have done its job.

17
00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:24.890
Each of these follows one rule.

18
00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:30.930
A good control matches the treatment in every step except the one being tested.

19
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:36.850
Without that match, the dull explanation and the interesting one predict the same

20
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:41.130
result. Then a difference between them can point to that step.

21
00:01:42.433 --> 00:01:47.533
A drug control gets the vehicle, the drug's solvent without drug, at the same

22
00:01:47.533 --> 00:01:54.193
concentration. Whoever scores the cells fixes positivity thresholds before analysis

23
00:01:54.193 --> 00:01:56.373
and scores blind to condition.

24
00:01:57.653 --> 00:02:02.713
Otherwise expectations can slip into the counts, one borderline cell at a time.

25
00:02:03.733 --> 00:02:05.493
Stretch itself must be checked.

26
00:02:06.833 --> 00:02:11.293
Measure the strain the cells actually feel, and analyze only the strained region.

27
00:02:12.673 --> 00:02:18.293
In the 2016 study, two biaxial devices reached about 8.5% and 15%.

28
00:02:18.293 --> 00:02:21.613
percent. Those results should not be merged.

29
00:02:22.893 --> 00:02:24.673
Each device is its own condition.

30
00:02:25.817 --> 00:02:31.557
Now predict. Suppose stretched cells stall, and you suspect DNA damage.

31
00:02:32.797 --> 00:02:35.457
You stain for a damage marker, and it stays blank.

32
00:02:36.757 --> 00:02:39.017
Does that blank marker show there was no damage?

33
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.300
Only if the assay could have seen damage.

34
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:54.160
Benham-Pyle and colleagues ran methyl methanesulfonate, a DNA-damaging chemical, as

35
00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:55.240
a positive control.

36
00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.920
It shows the assay detects damage when damage is present.

37
00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:05.340
Even then, one missing marker cannot rule out every kind of damage.

38
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:09.360
Return to the stretched dish and its unstretched twin.

39
00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:14.180
They share the handling, medium, vehicle and blinded scorer.

40
00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:17.040
The strain itself was measured.

41
00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:19.400
Only the stretch differs.

42
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:25.200
Now a difference in tracked divisions can point to stretch, for the period watched.
